  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 158 PM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nLow stratus continues to flow in from the north, obscuring \nskies. While there is considerable dry and subsiding air aloft, \na shallow layer of moisture exists between 925-850 mb. Soundings\nindicate this layer of moisture will persist through tonight \nand much of Friday. Weak instability in this layer may also \nallow for some light sprinkles to drizzle. No accumulation is \nexpected. Stratus will limit the area previously expected to see\na hard freeze tonight (28 deg F or less). After coordinating \nwith neighbors, have boosted overnight lows with a blend of NBM \nand RAP13. Overnight lows now range from 29 to 33, warmest over \nnorthwest Iowa. Should any area dip into 28 degrees or less it \nwill most likely occur along and north of Highway 14 in eastern \nSouth Dakota. Breezy northerly winds will continue to decrease \nthe remainder of this afternoon, becoming light and variable \nover night. Areas of low stratus/fog may briefly reduce \nvisibility to 1 mile or less. Confidence in fog formation is low\nwith the REFS indicating a less than 25% probability. Any fog \nshould burn off by mid-Friday morning.\n\nBy Friday afternoon weak upper ridging and a surface high pressure \npush in from the west. This will finally begin to break up the \nclouds late Friday afternoon as slightly stronger southerly winds \nwork into the low levels. While we will likely see peaks of \nsunshine, it will probably not be enough to boost our high \ntemperatures. Most areas will see low to mid 40s, while south \ncentral South Dakota may just squeak into the low 50s. By Friday \nevening the surface high will be centered over Iowa and weak low \nlevel moisture return will flow in from the south. At the same time \na shortwave and WAA will pass through the region. With little \ninstability or shear to speak of the most likely result is another \nround of low stratus. This will once again work to limit overnight \nlows to the low to mid 30s. Light and variable winds with surface \nconditions near saturation may allow for the stratus to lower and/or \nfog to develop. As flow aloft becomes northwesterly clouds and fog \nwill scour out by late Saturday morning. \n\nDry, mostly sunny skies, light southerly winds, and WAA will follow \nfor the rest of Saturday with highs climbing into the mid 50s to low \n60s over south central South Dakota. Similar conditions for Sunday \nand Monday will see highs climb into upper 50s to mid 60s both days. \nMonday afternoon a dry upper wave and cold front will pass through \nthe region. Winds will become northerly and temperatures will cool \ninto the 50s for Tuesday. Similar conditions continue into the \nlatter half of next week.",
